# Always code-review

After any code change, before committing, pushing, or opening/updating a PR,
review the diff with a code-review mindset and report the findings.

- Findings first, ordered by severity: bugs, behavioral regressions,
  security / product-wall breaks, missing tests. Style comes last, if at all.
- Cite file and line for each finding and say what the user-visible effect is.
- RedMed specifics to check every time:
  - Assist (`tapper/`): no Supabase, MCP, VPS, sign-in, Accept, or NFC; `#d=`
    codec and SOS invariants intact (see `AGENTS.md`).
  - Anything a stranger reads on the band card must not state a medical fact
    the band doesn't actually carry (e.g. "No" for an unset field).
  - Shell layout change → `CACHE` bumped in both `sw.js` and `tapper/sw.js`.
  - Gates: `bash scripts/sync-tapper.sh`, `node scripts/test-d-codec.mjs`,
    `node scripts/test-product-independence.mjs`.
- When the user asks only for a review, do not change code. Otherwise, fix
  what's clearly in scope and list the rest in the PR body under
  "Known issues".
- If there are no findings, say so in one line.
